  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n...TONIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY...\n\nEarly this afternoon, water vapor satellite and upper air analysis \nshow a building mid/upper ridge across the central CONUS. Meanwhile, \na potent deep layer trough is lumbering eastward towards the \nCalifornia coastline. Closer to home and at the surface, a weak \nsurface trough resides over southwest Kansas and the OK/TX \nPanhandles with southerly low level flow overspreading much of the \nCWA out ahead of it. Meridional flow aloft overspreading much \nof the southern and central Rockies will promote gradual \npressure falls across the Front Range and High Plains leading to\na tightening of the pressure gradient and resultant increasing \nsoutherly low-level flow through the rest of the week. The \nadvection of warmer temperatures will support highs each \nafternoon Thursday through Saturday in the mid to upper 70s. \nWith ample sunshine on Thursday, it's possible some locations \ncould surpass 80 degrees. Increasing cloud cover Friday and \nSaturday should keep temperatures somewhat in check, but they \nwill remain above average for this time of year.\n\n...BEGINNING OF NEXT WEEK...\n\nMid to long range deterministic and ensemble guidance continues\nto show the aforementioned deep layer trough continuing it's \nvery slow trek eastward towards the central plains late this \nweekend into early next week. Early on Sunday, continued \nsoutherly to southwesterly flow aloft over the southern and \ncentral Rockies will support weak lee cyclogenesis, and surface \ntrough/frontal boundary will begin to progress eastward into \nthe central plains. Additionally, moisture from the eastern \nPacific (likely in part from current TD 22-E) will be pulled \nnorthward into the central plains. With increasingly synoptic \nlift, increasing convergence at the surface with the sharpening \nfrontal boundary, and increasing moisture with PWATs well over \n1\" (around 90-95th percentile for this time of year), confidence\nis increasing for widespread rainfall across much of the \nwestern 2/3rds of Kansas late Sunday into Monday. By Tuesday, \nthe mid/upper trough driving this unsettled weather is forecast \nweaken and phase into the polar jet residing across the northern\nCONUS. With the loss of forcing for ascent, rain chances are \nlikely to taper off by late Monday or early Tuesday. With the \nslow progression of this particular system, it's likely some \nportions of eastern Kansas could miss out on rain altogether, \nthough it's too early to say that with much certainty at the\nmoment. Additionally, this system will bring in a cooler air \nmass with the current forecast calling for afternoon highs in \nthe 60s and overnight lows in the upper 40s to start off next \nweek.",
